**Connect-mode behaviour (the contract the tests pin):**
- Dial; on open write `{"type":"hello","bootToken"}`; wait for `{"type":"hello_ack"}` (consumed, never forwarded). Any other line before the ack is logged and dropped.
- First ack ever → spawn the OMP child and start its stdout pump. Every ack → flush the backlog in order, then go live. Daemon→child lines: `shutdown` closes the child's stdin (not forwarded); everything else is written to stdin as `line\n`.
- Dial failure, or a close before the ack (a rejected hello) → log `[worker-shim] daemon stream <host>:<port> unavailable (<message>); retrying in <ms>ms`, sleep `min(200 · 2^n, 5000)` ms where `n` counts consecutive failures, retry. A successful ack resets `n` to 0. A stream drop after the ack → backlog frames as they arrive (bounded to the newest 1000 with one drop log, exactly as socket mode), retry starting at 200 ms.
- Child exit → stop reconnecting, `end()` the current handle if any, resolve with the child's exit code. A child exit during a backoff sleep or an ack wait ends the loop immediately.
- Before the first ack no child exists; the shim keeps retrying forever (the daemon's boot watchdog is what retires an unregistered worker — same division of labour as the tmux shim that listens forever).

